      "text": "To break the problem down, we need to find out the distinct prime factors of the sum of the positive divisors of \\(400\\).\n\nFirst, let's understand the problem in a broader context:\nWe need to determine the prime factors of the sum of the divisors of \\(400\\).\n\n1. Calculate the divisors of \\(400\\):\n  \n   The divisors of \\(400\\) can range from \\(1\\) to \\(400\\).\n   \n   \\[\n   400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\n   \\]\n  \n   Using this form, we can determine the number of divisors efficiently by using the formula for the total number of divisors:\n   - The number of divisors \\(d(n) = (e_1 + 1) \\times (e_2 + 1) \\times \\cdots \\times (e_k + 1)\\), where \\(n = p_1^{e_1} \\times p_2^{e_2} \\times \\cdots \\times p_k^{e_k}\\).\n   \n   For \\(400 = 2^4 \\times 5^2\\),\n   \\[\n   d(400) = (4 + 1)(2 + 1) = 5 \\times 3 = 15\n   \\]\n   Thus, \\(400\\) has 15 divisors.\n\n2. Let \\(s\\) be the sum of all the divisors of \\(400\\):\n\n   Using the formula for the sum of divisors function, we have:\n   \\[\n   s = 400 \\left( 1 + \\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{1}{4} + \\frac{1}{5} + \\frac{1}{10} + \\frac{1}{20} + \\cdots \\frac{1}{2^{3}} + \\frac{1}{2^{4}} + \\cdots \\frac{1}{5^{1}} + \\frac{1}{5^{2}} \\right)\n   \\]\n\n3. Simplify \\(s\\):\n   \\[\n   400 \\left( \\sum_{i=0}^{\\infty} \\frac{1}{5^i} \\right) = 400 \\left( \\frac{1}{1 - \\frac{1}{4}} \\right) = 400 \\left( \\frac{4}{3} \\right) = \\frac{1600}{3} = 533.\\overline{3}\n   \\]\n   But then, only integer sums of \\(400\\)'s multiples are actually considered in terms of finite addends:\n   \\[\n   s = \\sum_{d|400} d = 1 + 2 + 4 + 5 + 8 + 10 + 16 + 20 + 25 + 32 + 40 + 50 + 80 + 100 + 125 + 160 + 200 + 250 + 400\n   \\]\n   The sum of these is:\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{931}\n   \\]\n\n4. Enumerating \\( (d_i)\\) distinct prime factors of \\( (d+16 )\\  \n   404: \\ 4 \\ 25 \n   The \\ dist ...\n   \\end{enumerate}",
