      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "First, we find the divisors of $400$. \nWe can start by factoring $400$ as $2^4 \\cdot 5^2$. \nThe divisors of $400$ are: $1, 2, 4, 5, 8, 10, 16, 20, 25, 32, 40, 50, 80, 100, 125, 160, 200, 250, 400$. \nNext, we find the sum of these divisors. \nWe can use the formula for the sum of divisors of a number $n$ with prime factorization $n = p_1^{e_1} \\cdot p_2^{e_2} \\cdot \\ldots \\cdot p_k^{e_k}$ as $\\sigma(n) = (1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \\ldots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \\ldots + p_2^{e_2}) \\ldots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \\ldots + p_k^{e_k})$. \nUsing this formula, we have \n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\sigma(400) &= (1 + 2 + 2^2 + \\ldots + 2^4)(1 + 5 + 5^2) \\\\\n&= (1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 5 + 25) \\\\\n&= (1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)(1 + 5 + 25) \\\\\n&= (49)(32) \\\\\n&= 1568.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nFinally, we find the number of distinct prime factors of $1568$. \nWe can factorize $1568$ as $2^6 \\cdot 7$. \nTherefore, the sum of the positive divisors of $400$ has $\\boxed{2}$ distinct prime factors.\nThe answer is: 2",
